          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When does guidance describe antivirals as worth considering for influenza?",
          "answer": "Guidance on the use of antivirals for influenza highlights that they are particularly worth considering for patients who are severely ill. There is observational evidence supporting their benefit in these cases, beyond the typical outpatient setting. \n\nHowever, guidance varies regarding antiviral use in low-risk outpatients. Some recommend antivirals as a reasonable option for reducing symptoms, while others are more restrictive. These reviews indicate that in low-risk patients, the effect on symptom duration is modest, and the impact on complications and hospitalization is not well established. Therefore, routine antiviral use in this group is not justified (source: National immunization advisory committee and infectious diseases society guidance).\n\nI could not cover specific antiviral medications or their modes of action, as that information was not provided in the retrieved documents.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
